<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Marca Geronis</h1><p class="st-meta">937 CE – 965 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The vast eastern march of Margrave Gero, from which Otto I's frontier against the Slavs between the Elbe and the Oder was controlled.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 937 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gero-the-great">Gero</a> as margrave on the eastern frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, over the Slavic lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saale">Saale</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and beyond. Gero extended German control in campaigns against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-the-hevelli">Hevelli</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lusici">Lusatians</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/widukind-of-corvey">Widukind of Corvey</a> reports that he killed some thirty Slavic chiefs at a banquet.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The march was a military command over tribute-paying Slavic peoples, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sorbs">Sorbs</a> among them, rather than a settled principality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gero-the-great">Gero</a> campaigned as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-river">Oder</a> and in 963 forced the Polish ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a> to pay tribute for land up to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warta">Warta</a>. He founded the convent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gernrode-abbey">Gernrode</a>, where he was buried.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gero-the-great">Gero</a> died in 965 Otto I divided his march into smaller units: the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-march">Northern March</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-meissen">Margraviate of Meissen</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/margraviate-of-lower-lusatia">March of Lusatia</a> (later Margraviate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-lusatia">Lower Lusatia</a>), and the marches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merseburg">Merseburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a>. Bishoprics founded in 968 under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/archbishopric-of-magdeburg">Archbishopric of Magdeburg</a> completed the new frontier order.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/marca-geronis.jpg" alt="Marca Geronis" width="320" height="418"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%A4chsische_Ostmark.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>937 CE – 965 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gero</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity (rulers), Slavic paganism (population)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Saxon, Sorbian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saxon Eastern March, March of Gero</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=951&amp;focus=marca-geronis" aria-label="Marca Geronis on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4888 427 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Marca Geronis lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5199 691h0M5306 661h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5208 683h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5208 683h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Germany. <a href="/atlas?year=951&amp;focus=marca-geronis">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
